        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Massive training datasets help deep neural networks achieve impressive performance in many realworld applications such as image classification (Deng et al., 2009), speech recognition (Chung et al., 2018), and machine translation (Vaswani et al., 2017). But, such training is computationally expensive. How to select a small subset of the training data that still achieves high model accuracy is attracting more and more attention in the machine learning community (Liang et al., 2022) and is characterized as the coreset selection problem. In this paper, we consider one-shot coreset selection that aims to select a subset of the training data that can later be used to train models while retaining high accuracy (Coleman et al., 2019; Toneva et al., 2018) 1 A favored way to select such coresets is to assign an importance score to each example and select more important examples to form the coreset (Paul et al., 2021; Sorscher et al., 2022). Recent importance-based coreset methods (Toneva et al., 2018; Coleman et al., 2019; Paul et al., 2021) propose various different importance metrics. Pruning unimportant examples with these metrics achieves better accuracy than random sampling with low pruning rates. However, surprisingly, these methods suffer a catastrophic accuracy drop under high pruning rates (Guo et al., 2022; Paul et al., 2021), performing significantly worse than models trained with randomly sampled subsets. This accuracy drop is a significant limitation as it limits the extent to which coresets can be reduced in size with these selection methods.\nIn this paper, we explore the reasons behind this catastrophic accuracy drop, both theoretically and empirically, and show that current coreset selection methods ignore an essential factor, data\n1A related coreset research direction is dynamic coreset selection. Dynamic coreset selection allows the coreset to be changed during training between epochs (Killamsetty et al., 2021b; Mirzasoleiman et al., 2020). Dynamic coreset selection is out of scope for our paper. We discussed more details in Section 5.\ncoverage, which is important for model accuracy. We propose a novel coreset selection method, called Coverage-centric Coreset Selection (CCS), that combines data coverage with importancebased sampling. We find that CCS significantly outperforms existing methods at high pruning rates. More specifically, we make the following contributions:\n1. We start by generalizing the result in (Sener & Savarese, 2017), which provided a probabilistic bound on model loss for a r-cover coreset, where r is the smallest radius around the coreset points that covers 100% of the training set, to a density-based characterization of the coreset in which the radius r is allowed to cover a percentage p of a density space. This density-based partial cover allows us to use a p-r curve to describe the trade-off between radius r and cover percentage p. We theoretically prove a bound on model loss as a function of r and p for a given coreset (Section 3.1).\n2. Inspired by the theoretical results, we propose a novel metric, the area under the p-r curve (AUCpr) (Section 3.1). Lower values of AUCpr are more desirable and suggest higher data coverage. Based on the proposed metric, we show that coresets selected by current coreset methods at high pruning rates can be expected to perform worse than random pruning (Section 3.2).\n3. We then investigate if factoring in coverage in importance-based sampling can help prevent catastrophic accuracy drop at high pruning rates. We propose the CCS method that adopts a classical idea from statistics: stratified sampling. Different from current methods that prune unimportant (easy) examples first, CCS, based on stratified sampling, ensures sampling budgets for data with different importance scores (Section 3.3).\n4. We evaluate CCS on four datasets (CIFAR10, CIFAR100 (Krizhevsky et al., 2009), SVHN (Netzer et al., 2011), and CINIC10 (Darlow et al., 2018)) and compare it with five other coreset selection methods: random, entropy (Coleman et al., 2019), forgetting (Toneva et al., 2018), EL2N (Paul et al., 2021), and AUM (Pleiss et al., 2020). Compared to these counterparts, we find that CCS achieves better accuracy with high pruning rates on all four datasets and comparable performance with low pruning rates. For example, with a 90% pruning rate, on CIFAR 10 (CIFAR100), CCS achieves 7.04% (10.83%) better accuracy than random sampling and at least 20.16% (26.63%) better than other baseline coreset methods 2 (Section 4).\nTo the best of our knowledge, our work is the first to study the data coverage problem of importancebased one-shot coreset selection and propose a one-shot coreset selection method that works well at both low and high pruning rates."
